Title: "Names"
Id: "207670b3-6b94-49e7-a5e8-2252477f4401"

Text: "A normal formulation name is either an identifier-like name or a stropped
symbolic name.

Identifier-like names match `[A-Za-z0-9](?:[A-Za-z0-9_']*[A-Za-z0-9'])?`, so they
may start with a digit, may contain `_` internally, and must otherwise end in an
ASCII letter, digit, or prime.

```text
x
x1
x_1
abc123
123
X'
x'_a'
```

A name may carry one or more trailing **prime marks** (`'`) after an
alphanumeric, including on a subscript. Primes render as LaTeX primes: `X'`
renders as $X'$ and `x'_a'` as $x'_{a'}$.

Stropped symbolic names wrap an operator name in backticks. The inside text is
non-empty and uses only the operator characters `-~!#%^&*\\+=|<>/`, optionally
with trailing primes:

```text
`*`
`*+`
`*'`
```

`` `*` `` is the operator `*` referred to **by name**, so where `*` is bound —
a magma's operation, for instance — `` `*` `` resolves to that operator's value.
It may be passed as an argument and invoked in function form as `` `*`(a, b) ``.
It renders as the operator itself, without the backticks: `` `*` `` as $*$ and
`` `*'` `` as $*'$.

Names may contain underscores internally, but a name ending in `_` or `__` is a
placeholder, not a normal name; `_x` and `'x` are not names at all.

In rendering, names of the form letters followed by digits are shown with a
numeric subscript, so `x1` renders like `x_1`.

The exact spellings `is`, `is?`, `is_not?`, `via`, `member_of`, and `satisfies`
are reserved by the lexer-driven formulation parser."
Id: "b1c37930-d8e1-4a73-8c04-21114dc5f2f1"
